.SH "DECODER PIXEL ASPECT"
The raster attribute command
.B "\[dq] Pan ; Pad ; Ph ; Pv"
sets the vertical pixel multiplier n to round(Pan/Pad), with halves rounded up
(5/2 becomes 3, 3/2 becomes 2). Ratios below one and missing or zero Pan or Pad
use one output row per SIXEL pixel. Horizontal dimensions are unchanged.
The initial multiplier is one; DCS macro P1 and grid size P3 do not change it.
.PP
Each command changes the multiplier for subsequent SIXEL characters, even
within a band. Earlier paint is never rescaled. A character's bit k paints
n rows starting at band_top + k*n, and '-' advances band_top by 6*n using
the multiplier current at that command. Changes can therefore produce
vertical gaps or overlaps. This models VT340 vertical pixel multiplication;
mid-image changes have not been verified on hardware.
.PP
With a constant multiplier of one, or a nonunit multiplier declared before
drawing, the optimized decoder paints at 1:1 and expands the final rows. A late nonunit declaration or
changing multipliers select a serial painter in output coordinates.
Repeated declarations with the same rounded multiplier retain the optimized
path. Drawing includes blank SIXEL characters, '!', '$' and '-'; color
commands may precede the first raster declaration.
.PP
Ph and Pv specify output pixels. For constant aspect, a partial final group
of repeated rows ends exactly at Pv when the data fits the declared raster.
The changing-aspect painter applies the ordinary trusted clipping or
untrusted growth rules directly to output rows. Existing size limits and
paint-outside-raster flags apply. Each sequence or animation packet selects
its own path before composition onto the retained output canvas.
.PP
Fast4 undither uses the source-resolution image before expansion on the
constant-aspect path. The changing-aspect path first paints an indexed image
in output coordinates, then applies ordinary non-fused fast4 reconstruction
without any further expansion.

